shadcn dependencies (button, input, textarea, avatar, dialog, select, switch, tooltip, sonner) and the npm packages `date-fns`, `motion` and `radix-ui` are installed with it. It needs schema v2 (`@sendo/schema`). Styling uses your theme tokens, light and dark.

## Setup

`inbox-config.ts` is the only file you have to edit:

| Export                    | What to do                                                                                                                                        |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `requireUser()`           | Return the signed-in user’s id and tenant from your auth, or redirect to your login. It throws until you implement it.                            |
| `inboxRealtimeEnabled()`  | Live updates through Supabase Realtime (on when the public Supabase env vars are set).                                                            |
| `inboxNav`                | Paths of the other Sendo screens you installed (`/inbox/numbers`, `/inbox/rules`, `/inbox/metrics`, `/inbox/links`); `null` hides the rail entry. |
| `resolveUserNames(ids)`   | Display names for note authors, approvers and the signed-in user. Use the same source as `resolveUsers` in `lib/sendo.ts`.                        |
| `contactAppHref(contact)` | Link for “Ver … en la app” from the contact’s `appRef`; `null` shows the reference as text.                                                       |
| `inboxAgentNames`         | Display names for your agents, e.g. `{ recepcion: "Recepción IA" }`.                                                                              |

Also configure `resolveRole` (and `resolveUsers`, `resolveAssignees`) in `lib/sendo.ts`: a user whose role resolves to `null` sees “No tienes acceso a la bandeja”.

## Server-action auth

Every server action calls your auth check first, and the template’s `requireUser()` throws until you implement it (fail closed). It then resolves the user as a Sendo actor through your `resolveRole`; every `sendo.inbox.*` call takes it and enforces the role matrix ([Team inbox](https://sendo.dev/docs/team-inbox#roles)):

```ts title="app/(protected)/inbox/actions.ts"
interface AppUser {
  id: string;
  tenantId: string | null;
}

// Fail closed: throw until you wire your app's session check.
async function requireUser(): Promise<AppUser> {
  throw new Error("TODO: requireUser() — return the signed-in staff user or throw.");
}

// The acting user: role and queues come from `resolveRole` in lib/sendo.ts.
// No `resolveRole`, or no role in the tenant, throws ForbiddenError (FORBIDDEN).
async function requireActor() {
  const user = await requireUser();
  return sendo.actor(user.id, user.tenantId);
}
```

```ts title="app/(protected)/inbox/actions.ts"
export async function listConversations(cursor?: string) {
  const actor = await requireActor();
  // Agents only get their own conversations and unassigned ones in their queues.
  return sendo.inbox.list({ actor, status: ["human", "agent"], cursor });
}

export async function openThread(conversationId: string, cursor?: string) {
  const actor = await requireActor();
  const page = await sendo.inbox.thread(conversationId, { actor, cursor });
  await sendo.inbox.markSeen(conversationId, { actor });
  return { ...page, items: page.items.toReversed() }; // newest first → chronological
}

export async function reply(conversationId: string, payload: InboxReplyPayload) {
  // Step-in: sends as the user; the status and the agent don't change.
  return sendo.inbox.reply(conversationId, payload, { actor: await requireActor() });
}

export async function take(conversationId: string) {
  return sendo.inbox.take(conversationId, { actor: await requireActor() });
}
```

- `reply` stores the message as sender `human` with `senderRef` = your user id, without changing the status. Outside the window, free-form replies throw `WindowClosedError` before calling Meta, and the composer switches to the template picker.
- A conversation of another tenant is `RECORD_NOT_FOUND`; one the user may not see or act on is `FORBIDDEN`.
- The page also computes what the role allows (`can(actor, …)`): the pause switch, reassigning and the agent cost are hidden from roles without them. The package checks again on every call.
- Media URLs in `thread` are signed for 300 seconds; request the thread again rather than storing them.

## The actions contract

`<Inbox actions={...} />` receives the server actions as one object. The page passes the ones in `actions.ts`; you can pass your own implementation (another backend, a test double). Every action resolves to a result instead of throwing, so the inbox can show the right message:

```ts title="components/sendo/types.ts"
export type InboxActionResult<T> =
  | { ok: true; data: T }
  | {
      ok: false;
      code:
        | "WINDOW_CLOSED"
        | "NOT_FOUND"
        | "INVALID"
        | "FORBIDDEN"
        | "ACCOUNT_INACTIVE" // the number was disconnected: "Número desconectado"
        | "CONFLICT" // duplicate snippet shortcut or tag name
        | "UNKNOWN";
      message: string;
    };

export type InboxTab = "open" | "mine" | "agent" | "human" | "closed";

export type InboxReplyInput =
  | { kind: "text"; body: string }
  | { kind: "media"; file: File; caption?: string }
  | { kind: "template"; name: string; language: string; variables: string[] };

type Result<T> = Promise<InboxActionResult<T>>;

export interface InboxActions {
  list(input: {
    tab: InboxTab;
    accountId?: string | null;
    tagIds?: string[]; // any-match
    search?: string; // name, app reference or phone
    cursor?: string | null;
  }): Result<InboxPage<InboxConversationView> & { counts: Record<InboxTab, number> | null }>;
  /** Newest first, notes, system lines and drafts included, plus display names. */
  thread(
    conversationId: string,
    cursor?: string | null,
  ): Result<InboxPage<InboxMessageView> & { names: Record<string, string> }>;
  reply(conversationId: string, input: InboxReplyInput): Result<InboxMessageView>;
  markSeen(conversationId: string): Result<null>;
  take(conversationId: string): Result<InboxConversationView>;
  assign(conversationId: string, userId: string | null): Result<InboxConversationView>;
  returnToAgent(conversationId: string): Result<InboxConversationView>;
  close(conversationId: string): Result<InboxConversationView>;
  pauseAgent(conversationId: string, paused: boolean): Result<InboxConversationView>;
  approveDraft(messageId: string, text?: string): Result<InboxMessageView>;
  discardDraft(messageId: string): Result<null>;
  addTag(conversationId: string, tagId: string): Result<InboxTag[]>;
  removeTag(conversationId: string, tagId: string): Result<InboxTag[]>;
  listAssignees(conversationId: string): Result<InboxAssignee[]>;
  listTags(): Result<InboxTag[]>;
  listTemplates(conversationId: string): Result<InboxTemplateView[]>;
  // Optional: the UI hides note mode, the snippet picker and the admin sheets without them.
  note?(conversationId: string, text: string): Result<InboxMessageView>;
  listSnippets?(conversationId: string | null): Result<InboxSnippetView[]>;
  renderSnippet?(
    snippetId: string,
    conversationId: string,
  ): Result<{ text: string; unresolved: string[] }>;
}
```

- `WINDOW_CLOSED` from `reply` switches the composer to the template picker and keeps what was typed; from `approveDraft` the draft card shows “La ventana cerró; usa una plantilla”.
- `ACCOUNT_INACTIVE` marks the number as disconnected: the banner appears and the composer is disabled. Reads keep working.
- `FORBIDDEN` from `list` shows “No tienes acceso a la bandeja”. On a single conversation (for example one reassigned away from an agent) it removes the conversation from the list and closes it.
- Failures show a `sonner` toast; successful actions don’t (the system line and the badge change are the feedback).

## Realtime and RLS

The migration adds `sendo_conversations` and `sendo_messages` to the `supabase_realtime` publication. Browser clients use your anon key and the user’s session, so they only receive rows for which `sendo_can_access_tenant(tenant_id)` is true. With the default (`false`) the inbox gets no live updates: implement it as in [Quickstart step 4](https://sendo.dev/docs/quickstart#migration).

Without Supabase Realtime (no public Supabase env vars, `inboxRealtimeEnabled()` returning `false`, a Drizzle or Prisma adapter, or a channel that errors or times out) the inbox polls: the list every 10 s and the open thread every 3 s, paused while the browser tab is hidden. New inbound messages in the open thread are marked seen, as with realtime.

A change patches the open list and thread right away, then the list is fetched again (previews, tags, assignee names, and visibility: a conversation reassigned away from an agent leaves their list). Tag changes made by someone else (`sendo_conversation_tags` isn’t in the publication) show up with that refetch.

## Strings

The inbox is in Spanish (es-GT) by default. Every user-facing string comes from one defaults module and can be overridden through a `strings` prop, so you can rephrase or translate without editing the components. Times use a 24-hour clock in the `America/Guatemala` timezone by default (`timeZone` prop).

| Group                     | Keys (examples)                                                                                                   |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Rail and access           | `navNumbers, navRules, navMetrics, navLinks, accessDeniedTitle`                                                   |
| Conversation list         | `tabOpen, tabMine, tabAgent, tabHuman, tabClosed, searchPlaceholder, numberAll, notLinked, flagDraft, flagPaused` |
| Thread header and banners | `pauseAgent, take, returnToAgent, close, handoffBannerTitle, pausedBannerBy(name), resume, disconnectedTitle`     |
| Messages                  | `fromPhone, importedMessage, approvedBy(name), origin(code), noteLabel(name), dayToday, actorAgent(name)`         |
| Draft card                | `draftTitle(agent), draftAwaiting, draftBeforeHandoff, draftApprove, draftSendEdited, draftWindowClosed`          |
| Context panel             | `sectionAssignment, queueLabel(queue), sectionTags, fieldOrigin, originDirect, sectionCost, costTurns(n)`         |
| Window                    | `windowRemaining(hours, minutes), windowClosed, windowNeverOpened`                                                |
| Composer and templates    | `composerModeReply, composerModeNote, composerHintStepIn, snippetUnresolved(names), templateExplanation`          |

The full list with defaults is `InboxStrings` / `defaultInboxStrings` in `components/sendo/strings.ts`. Some keys are functions (plurals, names):

```ts title="Overriding strings"
// <Inbox strings={{ ... }} /> — override any key; the rest keep the Spanish defaults.
export const inboxStrings = {
  tabHuman: "Con persona",
  take: "Asignarme",
  templateExplanation: "La ventana de 24 horas cerró. Envía una plantilla aprobada.",
  unreadCount: (count: number) => (count === 1 ? "1 nuevo" : `${String(count)} nuevos`),
};
```

## Motion tokens

Inbox components animate only `transform` and `opacity`, with durations and curves from these custom properties:

```css title="components/sendo/motion.css"
/* sendo inbox motion tokens. Override any of them in your own CSS. See MOTION.md. */
:root {
  --sendo-ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
  --sendo-ease-in-out: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);
  --sendo-dur-press: 120ms; /* press feedback 100–160 ms */
  --sendo-dur-fast: 160ms; /* tooltips, small popovers 125–200 ms */
  --sendo-dur-menu: 200ms; /* dropdowns, selects 150–250 ms */
  --sendo-dur-sheet: 320ms; /* dialogs, sheets 200–500 ms */
}

/*
 * Reduced motion keeps the durations, so opacity and colour still fade (they help to follow a
 * state change), and removes movement: every element that moves carries `sendo-motion-transform`,
 * whose transform (rise on enter, press scale) is neutralised here. The mobile sheets are driven
 * by `motion` and switch to an opacity-only fade through `useReducedMotion()`.
 */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .sendo-motion-transform {
    transform: none !important;
  }
}
```

| Token                                    | Used for                                                                                                                      |
| ---------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `--sendo-dur-press`                      | Press feedback on the rail, the thread actions, the draft buttons and send (scale 0.97 on pointer-down)                       |
| `--sendo-dur-fast`                       | Status badge and status tick cross-fades, tab and tag chip colours                                                            |
| `--sendo-dur-menu`                       | Rows, bubbles, notes and draft cards that arrive while you look; a banner that appears; the draft card leaving after approval |
| `--sendo-dur-sheet`                      | The context side panel (768–1179 px). The phone sheets use a critically damped motion spring with drag-to-dismiss instead     |
| `--sendo-ease-out / --sendo-ease-in-out` | Entering elements / on-screen movement. Never ease-in.                                                                        |

Override them after importing `motion.css`. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the durations stay, so opacity and colour still fade (they help to follow a state change), and movement is removed: every element that moves carries `.sendo-motion-transform`, whose transform is neutralised. The phone sheets read `useReducedMotion()` and only fade. The recipes and the reasons behind each one (and what was left out) are in the item’s `MOTION.md`.

```css title="app/globals.css"
/* app/globals.css — after importing components/sendo/motion.css */
:root {
  --sendo-dur-menu: 160ms; /* snappier entrances */
  --sendo-ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1);
}

/* Turn every inbox motion off (opacity and colour changes become instant too). */
.inbox-static {
  --sendo-dur-press: 0ms;
  --sendo-dur-fast: 0ms;
  --sendo-dur-menu: 0ms;
  --sendo-dur-sheet: 0ms;
}
```

Every `sendo.inbox.*` method takes the acting user as an `actor` and checks it on the server. The actor comes from `sendo.actor(userId, tenantId)`, which calls your `resolveRole`; Sendo stores no users or roles.

## Roles

`resolveRole({ userId, tenantId })` returns `{ role: "agent" | "supervisor" | "admin", queues? }` or `null` (no access). Without `resolveRole`, or with `null`, `sendo.actor` throws `ForbiddenError` (`FORBIDDEN`), so the inbox fails closed.

| Can                                                                         | Agent | Supervisor | Admin |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ---------- | ----- |
| See conversations assigned to them, plus unassigned ones in their queues    | ✓     | ✓          | ✓     |
| See every conversation of the tenant                                        |       | ✓          | ✓     |
| Reply (step in), leave notes, tag, approve drafts on conversations they see | ✓     | ✓          | ✓     |
| Take an unassigned conversation, return it to the AI, close it              | ✓     | ✓          | ✓     |
| Assign to someone else (or unassign), pause the AI on a conversation        |       | ✓          | ✓     |
| See model usage and cost in the thread                                      |       |            | ✓     |

An unassigned conversation without a queue is in the tenant's general queue and every agent sees it. A conversation of another tenant is `RECORD_NOT_FOUND`; one of the tenant the actor may not see, or an action their role lacks, is `FORBIDDEN` (`{ capability, role }`). An agent whose conversation was reassigned to someone else gets `FORBIDDEN` on the next action.

```ts title="app/(protected)/inbox/actions.ts"
interface AppUser {
  id: string;
  tenantId: string | null;
}

// Fail closed: throw until you wire your app's session check.
async function requireUser(): Promise<AppUser> {
  throw new Error("TODO: requireUser() — return the signed-in staff user or throw.");
}

// The acting user: role and queues come from `resolveRole` in lib/sendo.ts.
// No `resolveRole`, or no role in the tenant, throws ForbiddenError (FORBIDDEN).
async function requireActor() {
  const user = await requireUser();
  return sendo.actor(user.id, user.tenantId);
}
```

## Step in, take and assign

- `reply` sends as sender `human` (`senderRef` = the actor) and changes nothing else: an `agent` conversation stays with the AI, which sees the message as `[operator] …` on its next turn. A reply also discards the agent's pending draft.
- `take` assigns the actor and moves the conversation to `human`. `assign(id, userId | null)` emits `conversation.assigned { from, to, by }`.
- `assignees(id)` lists who the conversation can go to: `resolveAssignees({ tenantId, queue, account })` (falling back to `resolveHandoffTargets`), with names and avatars from `resolveUsers`.
- Handoffs, takes, assignments, returns to the AI, closes and pauses add a system line to the thread (`internal`, sender `system`, `metadata.kind = "system"`). Rules add theirs with `kind = "rule"`. See [System events](#system-events).

```ts title="app/(protected)/inbox/actions.ts"
export async function note(conversationId: string, text: string) {
  return sendo.inbox.note(conversationId, text, { actor: await requireActor() });
}

export async function pauseAgent(conversationId: string, paused: boolean) {
  // Supervisors and admins only (conversations.pause_agent).
  return sendo.inbox.pauseAgent(conversationId, paused, { actor: await requireActor() });
}

export async function approveDraft(messageId: string, text?: string) {
  return sendo.inbox.approveDraft(messageId, { actor: await requireActor(), text });
}

export async function assign(conversationId: string, userId: string | null) {
  // Anyone but yourself needs conversations.reassign (supervisors and admins).
  return sendo.inbox.assign(conversationId, userId, { actor: await requireActor() });
}
```

## System events

Every system line is a structured event, so the inbox can show it in the viewer's language. `thread()` returns it as `message.systemLine`:

```ts
interface InboxSystemLine {
  kind: "system" | "rule";
  event: SystemEventName | string | null; // SYSTEM_LINE_EVENTS
  by: string | null; // user id, "system", "business_app", "agent:<name>" or "rule:<key>"
  params: Record<string, string | number | boolean | null>;
}
```

The stored `text` is an English sentence, used for search and previews. To render a line, build the sentence from `event` + `params` in the viewer's locale (`by` resolves to a name the same way assignees do). If `event` is `null` or not a `SystemEventName`, show `message.text` instead. Rows written before structured events have only a Spanish `text`, and they still render that way.

| `event`         | `kind: "system"` params                                      | `kind: "rule"` params (always `ruleKey`)                     |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `handoff`       | `reason`, `assignedTo` + `assignedToName` when one assignee  | `queue`, `tags` (comma-separated), when the route sets them  |
| `assigned`      | `assignedTo`, `assignedToName` (`by === assignedTo`: a take) | `assignedTo`, `assignedToName`; `minutesWaiting` = escalated |
| `unassigned`    | none                                                         | none                                                         |
| `returned`      | none                                                         | `minutesWaiting`                                             |
| `closed`        | none                                                         | `afterDays`                                                  |
| `agent_paused`  | none                                                         | none                                                         |
| `agent_resumed` | `agentResumeFrom` (newest inbound message id)                | none                                                         |
| `routed`        | none                                                         | `queue`, `tags`                                              |
| `after_hours`   | none                                                         | none                                                         |
| `escalated`     | none                                                         | `minutesWaiting`                                             |

When `by` is a user, `params.byName` holds their name at the time of the change.

## Notes

`note(id, text, { actor })` stores an internal note: `internal = true`, sender `human`, status `sent`. It is never sent to WhatsApp, the AI never sees it, and it doesn't count as an answer to the contact.

## Snippets and tags (respuestas rápidas y etiquetas)

### Snippets

A snippet has a `shortcut` (1–32 of `a-z 0-9 _ -`, typed after `/` in the composer, without the slash), a `body` of up to 4,096 characters with `{{variables}}`, a `visibility` and an optional number (`accountId`; `null` = every number).

- `visibility: "tenant"` snippets are for the whole team; creating, editing or deleting them needs `snippets.manage_tenant` (admins).
- `visibility: "personal"` snippets belong to their author: only they see and edit them (admins included).
- Shortcuts are unique per tenant and owner; a taken one is `ConstraintViolationError` (`CONFLICT` in the inbox actions).

Variables come from three built-ins and your app:

| Variable       | Value                                                                          |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `{{nombre}}`   | The contact's WhatsApp profile name                                            |
| `{{telefono}}` | The contact's phone                                                            |
| `{{agente}}`   | The acting user's name, from `resolveUsers`                                    |
| anything else  | `resolveSnippetVariables({ contact, conversation, account })` in `createSendo` |

App values win over the built-ins. A variable with no value (missing, blank, or the hook failed — it's logged) stays as `{{name}}` in the text and is listed in `unresolved`. The inbox composer highlights it and keeps **Enviar** disabled until it's filled in or removed.

```ts title="lib/sendo.ts"
const sendo = createSendo({
  adapter: memoryAdapter(),
  appSecret: process.env.SENDO_META_APP_SECRET ?? "",
  verifyToken: process.env.SENDO_VERIFY_TOKEN ?? "",
  resolveToken: (account) => process.env[account.tokenRef],
  resolveRole: () => Promise.resolve({ role: "admin" }),
  // App values for snippets, merged over the built-ins {{nombre}}, {{telefono}} and {{agente}}.
  // Leave a variable out (or blank) and it stays highlighted in the composer, blocking the send.
  resolveSnippetVariables: async ({ contact }) => {
    const values: Record<string, string> = {};
    const saldo = await findBalance(contact.appRef);
    if (saldo !== null) values.saldo = saldo;
    return values;
  },
});
```

```ts title="ts"
// A team snippet (admins: `snippets.manage_tenant`); anyone can create `visibility: "personal"`.
const saldo = await sendo.snippets.upsert(
  {
    shortcut: "saldo",
    body: "Hola {{nombre}}, tu saldo pendiente es de {{saldo}}. Vence el {{fecha_limite}}.",
  },
  { actor: admin },
);

// The picker list: team snippets plus the actor's personal ones, usable on this number.
const usable: Snippet[] = await sendo.snippets.list({ actor: admin, accountId });
const matches = searchSnippets(usable, "/sal"); // accent- and case-insensitive

// Filled for one conversation. Variables without a value stay as written and are listed.
const filled = await sendo.snippets.render(saldo.id, { actor: admin, conversationId });
// filled.text       → "Hola María José, tu saldo pendiente es de Q 2,450.00. Vence el {{fecha_limite}}."
// filled.unresolved → ["fecha_limite"]
```

`sendo.snippets`: `list({ actor, accountId? })`, `get(id, { actor })`, `upsert(input, { actor })`, `delete(id, { actor })` and `render(snippetId, { actor, conversationId })` → `{ text, unresolved }`. Someone else's personal snippet, or another tenant's, is `RecordNotFoundError`. The helpers `searchSnippets`, `findSnippetVariables`, `fillSnippetVariables` and `normalizeSearchText` (plus `SNIPPET_BODY_MAX_LENGTH` and `TAG_NAME_MAX_LENGTH`) are pure; in client components import them from the client-safe `@quami/sendo/snippets` subpath, as the inbox item does (importing values from `@quami/sendo` would bundle the server runtime, `ai`).

In the inbox, typing `/` (or the ⚡ button) opens the picker: it searches shortcut and body ignoring accents and case, ↑ ↓ choose, Enter or Tab insert (through `render`), Escape closes. Everyone manages their personal snippets from **Respuestas rápidas** in the rail; admins also manage the team's.

### Tags

The tenant's catalog: a `name` (1–64 characters, unique ignoring case) and a `color` from the fixed palette `TAG_COLORS` (`gray`, `red`, `orange`, `amber`, `green`, `teal`, `blue`, `violet`, `pink`). Every role can read it and tag the conversations they see (`sendo.inbox.tags.add` / `remove`); changing the catalog needs `tags.manage` (admins).

```ts title="ts"
const urgente = await sendo.tags.upsert({ name: "urgente", color: "red" }, { actor: admin });
await sendo.inbox.tags.add(conversationId, urgente.id, { actor: admin });
const catalog = await sendo.tags.list({ actor: admin }); // every role can read the catalog
const using = await sendo.tags.usage(urgente.id, { actor: admin }); // ask before deleting
await sendo.tags.delete(urgente.id, { actor: admin }); // also removes it from every conversation
```

`sendo.tags`: `list({ actor })`, `upsert({ id?, name, color }, { actor })`, `delete(id, { actor })` and `usage(id, { actor })`. Deleting removes the tag from every conversation, so the inbox's **Etiquetas** sheet shows the usage count before it asks to confirm. The list filters by tag (any-match) and [metrics](https://sendo.dev/docs/metrics) can be cut by tag.

## Agent pause

`inbox.pauseAgent(id, paused, { actor })` (supervisors and admins) and `sendo.conversations.pauseAgent(id, paused, { by })` stop the AI on one conversation; `sendo.accounts.pauseAgent` stops it on a whole number. While paused, inbound messages are stored and count as unread, but no turn runs. A turn already running finishes.

On resume the AI doesn't catch up: it answers from the next inbound message on. Every change emits `conversation.agent_paused { conversation, paused, by }`.

## Drafts

With `mode: "draft"` on the agent, or `agentMode: "draft"` on the number (which wins), the agent runs its turn and its tools as usual, but stores the reply as a draft (`draft = true`, status `pending`) instead of sending it.

- A newer turn replaces the pending draft; a reply from a person discards it.
- `approveDraft(messageId, { actor, text? })` sends it as the agent, with `metadata.approvedBy` and `metadata.edited`. After the 24-hour window closed it throws `WindowClosedError` and the draft stays.
- `discardDraft(messageId, { actor })` deletes it.
- A draft proposed before a handoff stays for review with `handoffAfterDraft = true`.

Tools run during the turn even in draft mode: a tool that writes data writes it before anyone approves the reply.

## Disconnected numbers

When a conversation's number was disconnected, `reply`, `note` and `approveDraft` throw `AccountStateError` (`ACCOUNT_STATE`, `state: "inactive"`), and `InboxConversation.accountActive` is `false`. Reading the list and the thread keeps working.

## Usage and cost

Every agent turn stores `tokensIn` and `tokensOut` on its reply or draft. `costCents` comes from the provider's reported cost (OpenRouter's `providerMetadata.openrouter.usage.cost`), else from the agent's `pricing: { inputPerMTok, outputPerMTok }` (USD per million tokens), else it's `null`. `sendo.inbox.thread` returns these fields to admins only.

Rules decide what happens to a conversation without anyone touching it: who answers outside business hours, which queue a new conversation goes to, who gets it, what happens when nobody replies, and when it closes. They are a small, declared set of five types, stored in `sendo_rules` per tenant (optionally per number), and admins edit them on the **Reglas** screen without a deploy.

## Rule types

| Type             | Params                                                                                                                                                                                | Runs                                                          |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `business_hours` | `timezone` (IANA), `schedule` (`mon`…`sun`, each a list of `["HH:MM", "HH:MM"]` ranges, end exclusive, `24:00` = midnight), `holidays?` (`YYYY-MM-DD`), `outsideMessage`, `appliesTo` | On every inbound message and on the tick                      |
| `route`          | `match: { channel?, account?, source?, keywords? }`, `action: { queue?, tags?, to: "agent" \| "human", reason? }`                                                                     | On a new or reopened conversation; in order, first match wins |
| `auto_assign`    | `queue?` (omitted = any queue, `null` = no queue), `strategy: "least_open" \| "round_robin"`                                                                                          | When a conversation enters `human`, inside hours              |
| `escalate`       | `afterMinutes` (1–10 080, open minutes only), `queue?`, `action: "reassign" \| "notify" \| "return_to_agent"`                                                                         | On the tick                                                   |
| `auto_close`     | `afterDays` (1–365) without activity                                                                                                                                                  | On the tick                                                   |

- **Business hours.** Outside hours the after-hours message is sent at most once per conversation per closed period, and only while the 24-hour window is open (it isn't a template; otherwise it's logged). Escalation and auto-assign timers don't count closed time. With `appliesTo: "human"` the AI keeps answering and the message goes to conversations with a person; with `"all"` the AI is held too. A rule for one number wins over the tenant-wide one.
- **Routing.** Keywords match whole words in the first message, ignoring case and accents. `source` is the `[#code]` of a link (or `ad:<id>` for a Click-to-WhatsApp ad, see [Links](https://sendo.dev/docs/widgets#attribution)). The action sets the queue, adds tags (created in gray when missing) and hands off to a person with `by: "rule:<key>"` when `to: "human"`. No match keeps today's behaviour.
- **Every rule action is visible.** It adds a system line to the thread (`kind: "rule"`, e.g. `assigned` with `ruleKey` and `assignedTo`, stored as "Assigned to Ana López by rule «turno-cobranza»"; see [System events](https://sendo.dev/docs/team-inbox#system-events)) and emits `conversation.status`, `conversation.assigned` or `rule.applied` with `by: "rule:<key>"`; `notify` emits `escalation { conversation, rule, minutesWaiting }` for your app to turn into a notification.

A rule's `key` is its name: unique per tenant, quoted in those system lines, and what `rules sync` upserts by. Every `params` object is validated by the zod schemas exported from `@quami/sendo` (`ruleParamsSchemas`, `parseRuleParams`); a stored rule that fails them is ignored by the engine and shown as an error on the screen.

## Roles and permissions

`resolveRole` decides who can do what ([Team inbox](https://sendo.dev/docs/team-inbox#roles)). Rules are admin only (`rules.manage`): `sendo.rules.*` throws `FORBIDDEN` for other roles, and the screen shows "No tienes acceso a Reglas".

| Capability                                                                    | Agent | Supervisor | Admin |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ---------- | ----- |
| See conversations assigned to self, plus unassigned ones in own queues        | ✓     | ✓          | ✓     |
| See all conversations in the tenant                                           |       | ✓          | ✓     |
| Reply, step in, note, tag, approve drafts on visible conversations            | ✓     | ✓          | ✓     |
| Take an unassigned conversation, return it to the AI, close it                | ✓     | ✓          | ✓     |
| Reassign to someone else; pause the AI on a conversation                      |       | ✓          | ✓     |
| Reports and metrics                                                           |       | ✓          | ✓     |
| Numbers, rules, AI pause per number, tenant snippets, tag catalog, broadcasts |       |            | ✓     |

The package checks are the source of truth for every adapter; Supabase RLS mirrors them as defense in depth.

## Rules in code

Declare the rules your app ships with in a standalone module, so the CLI can load it without Next.js:

```ts title="lib/sendo-rules.ts"
// lib/sendo-rules.ts: no Next.js imports, so `sendo rules sync --config` can load it.
export const rules: RuleDefinition[] = [
  {
    key: "Horario oficina",
    type: "business_hours",
    params: {
      timezone: "America/Guatemala",
      schedule: {
        mon: [
