Then store them for a tenant:

```shell
npx @quami/sendo rules sync --config lib/sendo-rules.ts --tenant <uuid>
```

- Missing rules are inserted by `(tenant, key)` with origin `code`. Rules already there (for example, edited on the screen) are **never overwritten** unless you pass `--force`, which replaces type, params and number but keeps position and on/off.
- Every rule is validated first; one invalid rule exits 1 and nothing is written.
- The engine reads stored rules only, so run the sync after changing the file (for example, in your deploy step). Full flags in [CLI](https://sendo.dev/docs/cli#rules-sync).

## The Reglas screen

```shell
npx shadcn add @sendo/workflows
```

It adds the screen at `/inbox/rules` (`app/(protected)/inbox/rules`) and the tick route. Admins:

- see the rules in evaluation order, each with its type, number, a one-line summary and who edited it last; **Desde código** marks rules from `rules sync`;
- turn rules on and off, move them up and down (route rules are first-match-wins), and delete them (a code rule comes back on the next sync);
- create rules of the five types and edit them with a typed form per type: the business-hours editor has the time zone, a weekly schedule with several ranges per day, holidays, the after-hours message and who it applies to. Forms validate with the package schemas before saving, and the server validates again;
- see an invalid stored rule as an error row with the problems listed, and fix it in the form.

Every change records `updated_by` (the actor's `userId`). Before using the screen, implement `requireUser()` in `app/(protected)/inbox/rules/rules-config.ts` (it throws until you do) and configure `resolveRole`. Optionally implement `resolveUserNames` there to show "Editada por Ana". Texts can be overridden with `<RulesScreen strings={{ ... }} />`.

The screen calls `sendo.rules`, which you can also use directly:

```ts title="ts"
// What the rules screen's server actions call. Every method needs `rules.manage` (admins).
const list = await sendo.rules.list({ actor: admin }); // RuleView[]: StoredRule & { valid, error }
await sendo.rules.upsert(
  { key: "Cierre automático", type: "auto_close", params: { afterDays: 7 } },
  { actor: admin },
); // by (tenant, key); records updatedBy = admin.userId
const first = list[0];
if (first !== undefined) {
  await sendo.rules.toggle(first.id, false, { actor: admin });
  await sendo.rules.reorder(list.map((rule) => rule.id).reverse(), { actor: admin });
}
```

## The tick

`sendo.maintenance.tick()` runs, in order and within a 50-second budget: after-hours bookkeeping (conversations held outside hours go back to the agent at opening), escalations, auto-close, broadcast batches, a billing re-check for numbers without a payment method (hourly) and the event prune (daily). It takes the lock `tick` (120 s), so two overlapping calls never double-send: the second returns `{ skipped: true }`. Call it **every minute**.

```ts title="ts"
const report = await sendo.maintenance.tick(); // { now?, budgetMs? } for tests
if (!report.skipped) {
  console.log(report.steps.escalations.count, report.steps.autoClose.count, report.durationMs);
}
```

`workflows` ships the route `app/api/sendo/tick/route.ts`: `GET` or `POST`, `maxDuration = 60`, and `401` unless the request carries `Authorization: Bearer <SENDO_CRON_SECRET>` (Vercel's `CRON_SECRET` is accepted too). With no secret configured it always answers `401`. The response is the `TickReport` JSON.

```ts title="app/api/sendo/tick/route.ts"
// Runs Sendo's periodic work: after-hours bookkeeping, escalations, auto-close, broadcast batches,
// billing re-checks and the daily event prune (`sendo.maintenance.tick`). Call it every minute
// from Vercel Cron (vercel.json) or pg_cron (`net.http_post`); see the workflows docs.
import { createHash, timingSafeEqual } from "node:crypto";
import { sendo } from "@/lib/sendo";

export const runtime = "nodejs";
export const dynamic = "force-dynamic";
export const maxDuration = 60;

/** `SENDO_CRON_SECRET`, plus Vercel's `CRON_SECRET` (Vercel Cron sends it as the bearer token). */
function cronSecrets(): string[] {
  return ["SENDO_CRON_SECRET", "CRON_SECRET"]
    .map((name) => process.env[name]?.trim() ?? "")
    .filter((value) => value !== "");
}

const digest = (value: string) => createHash("sha256").update(value, "utf8").digest();

/** `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`, compared in constant time against every configured secret. */
function authorized(header: string | null, secrets: string[]): boolean {
  const token = /^Bearer (.+)$/i.exec(header?.trim() ?? "")?.[1];
  if (token === undefined) return false;
  const given = digest(token);
  return secrets.map((secret) => timingSafeEqual(given, digest(secret))).includes(true);
}

async function tick(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
  const secrets = cronSecrets();
  if (!authorized(request.headers.get("authorization"), secrets)) {
    if (secrets.length === 0)
      console.warn("[sendo] tick: set SENDO_CRON_SECRET to enable /api/sendo/tick");
    return Response.json({ error: "unauthorized" }, { status: 401 });
  }
  try {
    const report = await sendo.maintenance.tick();
    return Response.json(report);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("[sendo] tick failed", error);
    return Response.json({ error: "tick_failed" }, { status: 500 });
  }
}

export const GET = tick;
export const POST = tick;
```

### Vercel Cron

```json title="vercel.json"
{
  "crons": [{ "path": "/api/sendo/tick", "schedule": "* * * * *" }]
}
```

Vercel Cron sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`, so set `CRON_SECRET` in the project (or set both variables to the same value). Running every minute needs a plan that allows per-minute crons.

### Supabase pg_cron

Enable `pg_cron` and `pg_net`, keep the secret in Vault and post to the route every minute:

```sql title="sql"
-- Supabase: Database → Extensions → enable pg_cron and pg_net. Keep the secret in Vault.
select vault.create_secret('<the SENDO_CRON_SECRET value>', 'sendo_cron_secret');

select cron.schedule(
  'sendo_tick',
  '* * * * *',
  $$
  select net.http_post(
    url := 'https://<your-domain>/api/sendo/tick',
    headers := jsonb_build_object(
      'Authorization', 'Bearer ' || (
        select decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets where name = 'sendo_cron_secret'
      ),
      'Content-Type', 'application/json'
    ),
    body := '{}'::jsonb,
    timeout_milliseconds := 60000
  );
  $$
);

-- Check the last runs, or stop it:
select status, return_message, start_time from cron.job_run_details
  where jobid = (select jobid from cron.job where jobname = 'sendo_tick')
  order by start_time desc limit 5;
select cron.unschedule('sendo_tick');
```

Any other scheduler works the same way: one authenticated request per minute. The tick already prunes events once a day, so the separate prune job in [Retention](https://sendo.dev/docs/retention) is optional once the tick runs.

Every read and write in Sendo goes through a `SendoAdapter`. Pick the one that matches your stack; all of them pass the same contract suite (`runAdapterContract` from `@quami/sendo/adapters/contract`), so the pipeline, the inbox, rules, broadcasts and metrics behave the same on each.

| Adapter                                              | Database                                                           | Media by default           | Access control                                  |
| ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `supabaseAdapter` (`@quami/sendo/adapters/supabase`) | Supabase Postgres, through PostgREST with the service role         | `supabaseStorage` (bucket) | Package role checks, plus RLS for browser reads |
| `drizzleAdapter` (`@quami/sendo/adapters/drizzle`)   | Any Postgres 15+ through `drizzle-orm` (node-postgres/postgres-js) | none: pass a `MediaStore`  | Package role checks only                        |
| `prismaAdapter` (`@quami/sendo/adapters/prisma`)     | Any Postgres 15+ through `@prisma/client`                          | none: pass a `MediaStore`  | Package role checks only                        |
| `memoryAdapter` (`@quami/sendo/adapters/memory`)     | Process memory (tests, demos)                                      | in-memory map              | Package role checks only                        |

## Supabase

The default. Install the `schema` item (the SQL migrations) and pass a **service role** client: the `sendo_*` tables have RLS on and the helper functions are granted only to `service_role`.

```ts title="lib/sendo-adapter.ts"
import { supabaseAdapter } from "@quami/sendo/adapters/supabase";
import { createClient } from "@supabase/supabase-js";

// The SERVICE ROLE client (bypasses RLS). Server only.
const supabaseAdmin = createClient(
  process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL ?? "",
  process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY ?? "",
  { auth: { persistSession: false, autoRefreshToken: false } },
);

export const adapter = supabaseAdapter(supabaseAdmin, {
  storageBucket: "sendo-media", // private bucket
  signedUrlTtlSec: 300,
});
```

- Status ordering, locks, unread counts and the default-number swap run as Postgres functions from the migration (`sendo_update_message_status`, `sendo_mark_sent`, `sendo_acquire_lock`, `sendo_set_default_account`, …).
- Metrics read the `sendo_metrics_*` views.
- Media goes to the private `sendo-media` Storage bucket (`supabaseStorage`); `createSendo({ media })` replaces it, e.g. with `s3Storage`.
- The inbox gets live updates from Supabase Realtime, filtered by the RLS hooks you replace (`sendo_can_access_tenant`, …). See [Security](https://sendo.dev/docs/security).

## Drizzle

`drizzleAdapter({ db, media? })` runs Sendo on your own Postgres (Neon, RDS, Cloud SQL, self-hosted) through Drizzle. `db` is a `drizzle-orm/node-postgres` or `drizzle-orm/postgres-js` database; `drizzle-orm` is an optional peer, so install it (and `pg` or `postgres`) in your app.

### Set up the database

1. Install the items: `npx shadcn add @sendo/schema @sendo/drizzle-schema`.
2. On plain Postgres (not Supabase), run `lib/db/sendo-postgres-prelude.sql` once. The migrations mention three Supabase roles (in read-only RLS policies) and `auth.uid()`; the prelude creates stand-ins that grant nothing: NOLOGIN roles `anon`, `authenticated`, `service_role` and an `auth.uid()` that returns null. It needs `CREATEROLE`; on Supabase it changes nothing.
3. Apply `20261010000000_sendo_v1.sql`, `20261011000000_sendo_v2.sql` and `20261013000000_sendo_v3.sql`, in that order, with your migration tool (for drizzle-kit, paste each into a `drizzle-kit generate --custom` migration). Postgres 15 or later (`nulls not distinct`).
4. Connect as the role that owns the tables (it needs to read and write every `sendo_*` table).

The SQL migrations are the source of truth. `lib/db/sendo-schema.ts` mirrors them as `pgTable` definitions (a test checks table and column names, types and nullability against the SQL) so you can join Sendo's tables in your own typed queries. Keep it out of the `schema` list in `drizzle.config.ts` so drizzle-kit never generates or pushes migrations for `sendo_*` tables.

### Create the adapter

```ts title="lib/sendo-adapter.ts"
import { drizzleAdapter } from "@quami/sendo/adapters/drizzle";
import { drizzle } from "drizzle-orm/node-postgres";
import { Pool } from "pg";

// Sendo's storage on your own Postgres through Drizzle. Use `adapter` in lib/sendo.ts:
//   createSendo({ adapter, media, ... })
// `sendo doctor --config lib/sendo-adapter.ts` and `sendo mcp --config ...` read `adapter`
// (and `media`) from this file.
//
// Already have a Drizzle `db`? Import it instead of creating a second pool:
//   export const adapter = drizzleAdapter({ db });

const pool = new Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL });

/** The Drizzle database Sendo uses (node-postgres). */
export const db = drizzle({ client: pool });

/** Sendo's `SendoAdapter` over the `sendo_*` tables of `DATABASE_URL`. */
export const adapter = drizzleAdapter({ db });

// Media (images, audio, documents) needs a MediaStore; there is no Supabase Storage here.
// For S3, R2 or any S3-compatible store (npm install @aws-sdk/client-s3 @aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner):
//   import { S3Client } from "@aws-sdk/client-s3";
//   import { s3Storage } from "@quami/sendo/storage/s3";
//   export const media = s3Storage({ client: new S3Client({}), bucket: process.env.SENDO_MEDIA_BUCKET! });
```

Then `createSendo({ adapter, media, ... })` in `lib/sendo.ts`. Already have a Drizzle `db`? Pass it instead of opening a second pool. `sendo doctor --config lib/sendo-adapter.ts` reads the same exports.

### How it works

- Plain SQL over the `sendo_*` tables (no stored functions, so no `EXECUTE` grants are needed). Rows come back as JSON, so `node-postgres` and `postgres-js` give identical results.
- The Supabase functions' work is done as single statements or transactions with the **same advisory locks** (`sendo_meta:<meta id>`, `sendo_default_account:<tenant>`): forward-only status updates and status placeholders (`updateStatus`), the placeholder merge in `markSent`, locks (`insert … on conflict … where expires_at <= clock_timestamp()`), unread increments, the default-number swap, and metadata and rule-state merges (`jsonb ||`). A Supabase app and a Drizzle worker can share one database.
- `sendo.metrics.*` runs `METRICS_SQL` (the same queries as the `sendo_metrics_*` views) with bound parameters.
- Failures the adapter doesn't map to a Sendo error throw `DrizzleAdapterError` (`operation`, Postgres `code`, `constraint`, `detail`).

## Prisma

`prismaAdapter({ prisma, media? })` runs Sendo on your own Postgres through the `PrismaClient` your app already has (Prisma 5 or later; with Prisma 7, a client built with a driver adapter such as `@prisma/adapter-pg`). `@prisma/client` is an optional peer, so it comes from your app.

### Set up the database

1. Install the items: `npx shadcn add @sendo/schema @sendo/prisma-schema`. You get the SQL migrations in `supabase/migrations/`, plus `prisma/sendo.prisma` and `prisma/sendo-postgres-prelude.sql`.
2. On plain Postgres (not Supabase), run `prisma/sendo-postgres-prelude.sql` once (the same prelude as the [Drizzle](#drizzle-database) item: NOLOGIN stand-ins for `anon`, `authenticated`, `service_role` and an `auth.uid()` that returns null).
3. Apply the three `schema` files in order, each as its own Prisma migration: `npx prisma migrate dev --create-only --name sendo_v1`, paste `20261010000000_sendo_v1.sql` into the new `migration.sql`, then the same for v2 and v3, then `npx prisma migrate dev`. Postgres 15 or later.
4. Add the models: use Prisma's multi-file schema (`schema: "prisma"` in `prisma.config.ts`) so `prisma/sendo.prisma` is picked up, or paste its blocks into your `schema.prisma`. Then `npx prisma generate`.

The SQL migrations are the source of truth. `sendo.prisma` mirrors them (models mapped with `@@map` / `@map` to the snake*case names, enums, relations, unique constraints and plain indexes); tests check it against the SQL (tables, columns, types, nullability) and check that `prisma migrate diff` from a migrated database to these models is empty, so Prisma Migrate never generates changes for `sendo*\*` tables. Prisma can't express the CHECK constraints, the partial and expression indexes, RLS, the functions or the metrics views; they stay in the SQL and Migrate leaves them alone.

### Create the adapter

```ts title="lib/sendo-adapter.ts"
import { prismaAdapter } from "@quami/sendo/adapters/prisma";
import { s3Storage } from "@quami/sendo/storage/s3";
import { S3Client } from "@aws-sdk/client-s3";
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma"; // your PrismaClient

/** Sendo's `SendoAdapter` over the `sendo_*` tables, through your Prisma client. */
export const adapter = prismaAdapter({ prisma });

/** Media (images, audio, documents): there is no Supabase Storage here. */
export const media = s3Storage({ client: new S3Client({}), bucket: process.env.SENDO_MEDIA_BUCKET! });
```

Then `createSendo({ adapter, media, ... })` in `lib/sendo.ts`. `sendo doctor --config lib/sendo-adapter.ts` reads the same exports. Options:

- `schema`: the Postgres schema that holds the `sendo_*` tables (default `public`), e.g. when you keep them in their own schema.
- `transaction`: `{ maxWait, timeout }` in ms for the interactive transactions (default 10000 / 15000).

### How it works

- Only `$queryRawUnsafe`, `$executeRawUnsafe` and `$transaction` are used, always with bound parameters (`$1`, `$2`, …; values are never spliced into the SQL). The adapter doesn't depend on your generated models, so renaming them or adding relations to your own models is safe.
- Rows are read as JSON (`to_jsonb`), so Prisma 5, 6 and 7, with or without a driver adapter, return the same values.
- The Supabase functions' work runs as single statements or interactive transactions with the **same advisory locks** as the SQL functions (`sendo_meta:<meta id>`, `sendo_default_account:<tenant>`): forward-only status updates and placeholders, the placeholder merge in `markSent`, locks, unread increments, the default-number swap, rule reordering, purge, and `jsonb ||` merges. A Supabase app and a Prisma worker can share one database.
- `sendo.metrics.*` runs `METRICS_SQL` with bound parameters.
- Failures the adapter doesn't map to a Sendo error throw `PrismaAdapterError` (`operation`, Postgres `code`).

## Shared: schema, media and access control

### Schema versions

Every adapter reads the same `sendo_*` tables, created by the `schema` migrations. `sendo_schema_meta` records the installed version; the package warns at startup and `sendo doctor` fails `schema-version` when it is behind, naming the files to apply. New Sendo versions only add migrations: apply them with whatever tool you use.

### Media

Media bytes never go through the adapter's tables, only through a `MediaStore`: `createSendo({ media })` wins, else the adapter's default (`adapter.storage`). The Supabase adapter defaults to its Storage bucket; Drizzle and Prisma have no default, so pass `media` (for example `s3Storage` from `@quami/sendo/storage/s3`, which works with S3, R2 and MinIO) or inbound images, audio and documents are stored as `failed`. See [API reference](https://sendo.dev/docs/api-reference#media-store).

### Access control

> **Note:**
>
> On the Drizzle and Prisma adapters, access control relies entirely on the package's role checks. RLS exists only on Supabase, and only for browser clients: your server connects as the table owner, which RLS does not restrict.

- Every inbox, metrics, broadcast, rule, snippet and tag call takes an `actor` (`sendo.actor(userId, tenantId)`) and checks its role and capabilities, on every adapter. That is the security boundary on Drizzle and Prisma.
- Never query `sendo_*` tables on behalf of a user without your own tenant and role check, and never expose them to browsers.
- Without Supabase Realtime the inbox polls instead (list every 10 s, open thread every 3 s, paused while the tab is hidden). See [Inbox](https://sendo.dev/docs/inbox).

### Your own adapter

Implement `SendoAdapter` and run the contract suite in your tests; it is the definition of done.

```ts title="my-adapter.contract.test.ts"
// my-adapter.contract.test.ts (run with Vitest)
import { runAdapterContract } from "@quami/sendo/adapters/contract";
import type { SendoAdapter } from "@quami/sendo";

declare function createMyAdapter(): Promise<SendoAdapter>;
declare function truncateTables(): Promise<void>;
declare const databaseReachable: boolean;

runAdapterContract("my-adapter", () => createMyAdapter(), {
  skip: databaseReachable ? false : "database unreachable",
  afterEach: truncateTables,
});
```

## sendo_events (built in)

Every webhook POST is stored raw in `sendo_events` so failed events can be reprocessed. Rows older than 30 days should be deleted. Pick one of the two options below; running both is harmless.

## Option A: pg_cron

If the `pg_cron` extension is enabled when the `schema` migration runs (Supabase: **Database › Extensions**), the migration schedules a daily job:

```sql title="sql"
-- Scheduled by the schema migration when pg_cron is installed:
select cron.schedule('sendo_prune_events_daily', '17 3 * * *', 'select public.sendo_prune_events(30)');
```

`sendo_prune_events(days)` deletes events received more than `days` ago and returns the count. It is service-role only. If you enable pg_cron later, or want another period:

```sql title="sql"
-- Enabled pg_cron after applying the migration? Schedule it yourself:
create extension if not exists pg_cron;
select cron.schedule('sendo_prune_events_daily', '17 3 * * *', 'select public.sendo_prune_events(30)');

-- Change the retention, inspect or remove the job:
select cron.unschedule('sendo_prune_events_daily');
select cron.schedule('sendo_prune_events_daily', '17 3 * * *', 'select public.sendo_prune_events(14)');
select * from cron.job where jobname = 'sendo_prune_events_daily';
```

## Option B: Vercel Cron

Without pg_cron, call `sendo.maintenance.prune()` from a scheduled route. It deletes events older than `eventRetentionDays` (default 30) and returns the count; pass `{ olderThanDays }` to override.

```ts title="app/api/cron/sendo-prune/route.ts"
// app/api/cron/sendo-prune/route.ts
import { sendo } from "@/lib/sendo";

export const runtime = "nodejs";

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  // Vercel Cron sends `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`.
  const secret = process.env.CRON_SECRET;
  if (!secret || request.headers.get("authorization") !== `Bearer ${secret}`) {
    return new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 });
  }
  const deleted = await sendo.maintenance.prune(); // events older than eventRetentionDays
  return Response.json({ deleted });
}
```

```json title="vercel.json"
{
  "crons": [{ "path": "/api/cron/sendo-prune", "schedule": "17 3 * * *" }]
}
```

Set `CRON_SECRET` in the Vercel project; Vercel sends it as a bearer token on cron requests. Any other scheduler (GitHub Actions, Supabase scheduled functions) can call the same route.

## Messages and media (your policy)

Sendo never deletes `sendo_messages`, `sendo_media` or stored files. How long conversations are kept is your app’s decision (and, for health or financial data, often a legal one). A starting point that deletes files through the Storage API and then the messages (their `sendo_media` rows cascade):

```ts title="app/api/cron/sendo-message-retention/route.ts"
// app/api/cron/sendo-message-retention/route.ts — YOUR policy for sendo_messages / sendo_media.
import { createClient } from "@supabase/supabase-js";

const RETENTION_DAYS = 365;
const BUCKET = process.env.SENDO_STORAGE_BUCKET ?? "sendo-media";

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  if (request.headers.get("authorization") !== `Bearer ${process.env.CRON_SECRET ?? ""}`) {
    return new Response("Unauthorized", { status: 401 });
  }
  const supabase = createClient(
    process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL ?? "",
    process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY ?? "",
    { auth: { persistSession: false } },
  );
  const cutoff = new Date(Date.now() - RETENTION_DAYS * 86_400_000).toISOString();

  // 1. Delete the stored files first (Storage API, not SQL on storage.objects).
  const { data: media, error } = await supabase
    .from("sendo_media")
    .select("storage_path")
    .lt("created_at", cutoff)
    .not("storage_path", "is", null)
    .limit(1000);
  if (error) throw error;
  const paths = (media as { storage_path: string }[]).map((row) => row.storage_path);
  if (paths.length > 0) await supabase.storage.from(BUCKET).remove(paths);

  // 2. Delete old messages; sendo_media rows cascade.
  const { count } = await supabase
    .from("sendo_messages")
    .delete({ count: "exact" })
    .lt("created_at", cutoff);
  return Response.json({ files: paths.length, messages: count ?? 0 });
}
```

> **Note:**
>
> - Delete files before rows: once the `sendo_media` row is gone you lose the storage path.
> - The sample handles up to 1,000 files per run; schedule it often enough or loop.
> - Deleting messages also removes them from the inbox and from agent history. Archive first if you need an audit trail. See [Security](https://sendo.dev/docs/security).

## Accounts and tenants

One deployment can serve many WhatsApp numbers. Each number is a `sendo_accounts` row with its `phone_number_id`, `waba_id`, its own token env var (`token_ref`), a `timezone` and an optional `tenant_id` (for example one number per clinic or building). Every contact, conversation and message copies the account’s `tenant_id`.

- Contacts are unique per account and phone: the same person writing to two tenants’ numbers becomes two contacts, each with its own history.
- `sendo.accounts.disconnect(id)` stops routing and sending for a number without deleting its data ([Numbers and tenants](https://sendo.dev/docs/numbers-and-tenants#disconnect)).

## Adding numbers

```shell
# .env.local / deployment env
SENDO_TOKEN_CLINICA_NORTE=...
SENDO_TOKEN_CLINICA_SUR=...

npx @quami/sendo accounts add --phone-number-id 111111111111111 --token-env SENDO_TOKEN_CLINICA_NORTE \
  --tenant-id 6f1c0c3e-0000-4000-8000-000000000001 --timezone America/Guatemala --yes
npx @quami/sendo accounts add --phone-number-id 222222222222222 --token-env SENDO_TOKEN_CLINICA_SUR \
  --tenant-id 6f1c0c3e-0000-4000-8000-000000000002 --yes
```

Numbers in different WABAs can use different tokens; numbers in the same WABA can share one env var. Then subscribe each WABA’s webhook ([Meta setup](https://sendo.dev/docs/meta-setup#webhook)) and run `doctor`, or `doctor --account <id>` for one.

## Inbound routing

All numbers share one webhook URL. Each change in a payload carries `metadata.phone_number_id`, which is looked up in `sendo_accounts`:

- Active match: processed for that account and tenant.
- No match or inactive: the event is marked `unrouted`, a warning is logged, the `event.unrouted` event fires, and nothing else is written. Add the account and `sendo.events.reprocess(id)` it.

```ts title="ts"
// Replies to inbound messages go out from the number that received them.
sendo.on("message", async ({ account, reply }) => {
  console.log("received on", account.displayPhone, "tenant", account.tenantId);
  await reply.text("Recibido");
});
```

## Sending from a number

With exactly one active account, sends use it by default. With more than one, every `sendo.send.*` and `sendo.templates.sync` call must pass `tenant` (the organization’s default number, see [Numbers and tenants](https://sendo.dev/docs/numbers-and-tenants#tenant)) or `account`: the Sendo account id, the Meta phone number id, or the `Account` object. Otherwise the call throws `AmbiguousAccountError` (or `AccountNotFoundError` for an unknown or inactive one). Replies (`reply`, agents, `sendo.inbox.reply`) always go out from the conversation’s own number.

```ts title="ts"
// With more than one active account, every send names its account:
// the sendo account id (uuid), the Meta phone_number_id, or the Account object.
await sendo.send.template(patientPhone, "recordatorio_cita", {
  account: tenantPhoneNumberId,
  language: "es",
  variables: ["Ana", "mañana 10:00"],
});
await sendo.send.text(patientPhone, "Te esperamos.", { account: tenantPhoneNumberId });
await sendo.templates.sync({ account: tenantPhoneNumberId });
```

```ts title="ts"
try {
  await sendo.send.text(patientPhone, "Hola"); // no account
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof AmbiguousAccountError) console.error(error.candidates); // account ids
}
```

## Agents per number

An agent’s `accounts` option is a list of account ids / phone number ids or a predicate; `tenants` filters by organization the same way, and both must match ([one agent for many tenants](https://sendo.dev/docs/numbers-and-tenants#agents)). Only one agent may match each account: overlapping lists throw `ConfigError` at registration; overlaps found later (predicates, new accounts) are logged and no agent answers on that account until you fix them. The prompt uses the account’s `timezone` unless the agent sets one.

```ts title="ts"
const model = "openai/gpt-5-mini";
const surTenantId = "6f1c0c3e-0000-4000-8000-000000000002";

sendo.agent({ name: "norte", accounts: ["111111111111111"], model, instructions: "…" });
sendo.agent({
  name: "sur",
  accounts: (account) => account.tenantId === surTenantId,
  model,
  instructions: "…",
});
```

Full agent options: [API reference](https://sendo.dev/docs/api-reference#agents).

## Access per tenant

- Browser reads (inbox realtime) are filtered by `sendo_can_access_tenant(tenant_id)`: implement it with your membership table ([Quickstart step 4](https://sendo.dev/docs/quickstart#migration)).
- Server actions pass the user’s `tenantId` to `sendo.inbox.*`, and `linkContact` / `resolveHandoffTargets` receive the `account` so you can scope lookups by `account.tenantId`.

A SaaS connects one or more WhatsApp numbers per organization (tenant). Sendo resolves which number to use from the tenant, so your code passes `{ tenant }` instead of account ids. For the data model and inbound routing, see [Multi-number](https://sendo.dev/docs/multi-number).

## Sending as a tenant

Every account-scoped call takes `tenant` next to `account`: all of `sendo.send.*`, `sendo.templates.sync` and `sendo.templates.list`. `tenant: null` means the accounts without a tenant.

```ts title="ts"
// Goes out from the organization's default number. No account ids to pass around.
await sendo.send.template(patientPhone, "recordatorio_cita", {
  tenant: tenantId,
  language: "es",
  variables: ["Ana", "mañana 10:00"],
});
await sendo.send.text(patientPhone, "Te esperamos.", { tenant: tenantId });
await sendo.templates.sync({ tenant: tenantId }); // every active number of the tenant
```

- `templates.sync({ tenant })` pulls the templates of every active number of the tenant; `templates.list({ tenant })` lists them.
- Replies (`reply`, agents, `sendo.inbox.reply`) always go out from the conversation’s own number; they don’t need either option.

## Resolution order

| You pass  | Sendo uses                                                                                                                                                        |
| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `account` | That account (id, phone number id or `Account`). With `tenant` too, an account of another tenant throws `AccountNotFoundError`.                                   |
| `tenant`  | The tenant’s **default** active number; without a default, its only active number. Several and no default: `AmbiguousAccountError`. None: `AccountNotFoundError`. |
| neither   | The only active number of the deployment; several: `AmbiguousAccountError`, whose message suggests passing `{ tenant }`.                                          |

`AmbiguousAccountError` carries `candidates` (only that tenant’s account ids when `tenant` was given) and `tenantId`. `AccountNotFoundError` carries `account` and `tenantId`.

```ts title="ts"
try {
  await sendo.send.text(patientPhone, "Hola", { tenant: tenantId });
} catch (error) {
  // Two or more active numbers in the tenant and none is the default.
  if (error instanceof AmbiguousAccountError) console.error(error.tenantId, error.candidates);
}
```

## Default number

Each tenant has at most one default number (the accounts without a tenant count as one tenant); the database enforces it with a unique index.

- The first number added to a tenant becomes its default (`accounts.add`, Embedded Signup).
- `sendo.accounts.setDefault(id)` moves the default atomically: the old one is unset and the new one set in one transaction, so concurrent calls never leave two defaults.
- When the default is disconnected and exactly one active number is left, that one becomes the default; otherwise the tenant has none until you pick one.
- Each change emits `account.default`.

## sendo.accounts

| Method                                        | Does                                                                                                                                            |
| --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `list({ tenant?, includeInactive? })`         | Accounts, oldest first. Disconnected ones only with `includeInactive`.                                                                          |
| `get(id)`                                     | One account, connected or not.                                                                                                                  |
| `add(input)`                                  | Registers a number (the code version of `sendo accounts add`). A number disconnected in the same tenant is reconnected with its id and history. |
| `setDefault(id)`                              | Makes it the tenant’s default. Disconnected numbers throw `AccountStateError`.                                                                  |
| `setLabel(id, label)`                         | Name shown in the inbox (e.g. "Recepción"); blank clears it.                                                                                    |
| `pauseAgent(id, paused, { by? })`             | Stops (or resumes) AI turns on every conversation of the number.                                                                                |
| `setAgentMode(id, "auto" \| "draft" \| null)` | Per-number agent mode; `null` keeps the agent definition’s own mode.                                                                            |
| `disconnect(id, { by? })`                     | See [Disconnecting](#disconnect).                                                                                                               |
| `purge(id)`                                   | See [Deleting an organization’s data](#purge).                                                                                                  |
| `refreshBilling(id)`                          | Re-reads the payment method on Meta (Embedded Signup).                                                                                          |
| `completeEmbeddedSignup(input)`               | Finishes Embedded Signup from the numbers screen.                                                                                               |

Every `id` can also be the Meta phone number id. An unknown one throws `AccountNotFoundError`. These are server-side methods: check the actor’s `numbers.manage` capability in your server actions before calling them.

```ts title="ts"
const numbers = await sendo.accounts.list({ tenant: tenantId, includeInactive: true });
const added = await sendo.accounts.add({
  tenantId,
  phoneNumberId: "333333333333333",
  wabaId: "444444444444444",
  displayPhone: "+502 5555 3333",
  tokenRef: "SENDO_TOKEN_CLINICA_ESTE", // env var NAME, never the token
  label: "Recepción",
}); // the tenant's first number becomes its default
await sendo.accounts.setDefault(added.id); // atomic swap within the tenant
await sendo.accounts.setLabel(added.id, "Recepción principal");
await sendo.accounts.pauseAgent(added.id, true, { by: "user_123" });
await sendo.accounts.setAgentMode(added.id, "draft"); // null = the agent's own mode
```

## Disconnecting

`disconnect(id)`:

1. sets `active = false` and clears `is_default`; inbound webhooks for the number become `unrouted` and sends throw `AccountNotFoundError`;
2. promotes the tenant’s only remaining active number to default, if there is exactly one;
3. for Embedded Signup numbers, unsubscribes your app from the WABA (`DELETE /{waba_id}/subscribed_apps`), best effort: a failure is logged, never thrown;
4. calls your `deleteToken(tokenRef)` hook, if configured (a failure is logged);
5. keeps contacts, conversations and messages, so the inbox still shows the history, read-only;
6. emits `account.disconnected` and, when the default changed, `account.default`.

Connecting the same number again in the same tenant reuses the row: same account id, the old conversations come back, with the new `tokenRef`. Connecting it from another tenant throws `DuplicateAccountError` (`ACCOUNT_EXISTS`).

> **Note:**
>
> `deleteToken` receives every disconnected account’s `tokenRef`, including env var names of numbers added by hand. Ignore refs your hook didn’t create; for example, a Vault-backed hook deletes only `vault:` refs.

## Deleting an organization’s data

`purge(id)` deletes a **disconnected** account and, by cascade, its contacts, conversations, messages, media rows and templates, then deletes the media objects from storage. It throws `AccountStateError` (`ACCOUNT_STATE`) on an active account and deletes nothing, so the order is always `disconnect` then `purge`. There is no undo; when to call it is your app’s decision.

```ts title="ts"
// Stops sending and routing, keeps the history readable, promotes the tenant's
// only remaining number to default, calls deleteToken(tokenRef).
await sendo.accounts.disconnect(accountId, { by: "user_123" });

// Later, when the organization asks for its data to be deleted:
try {
  await sendo.accounts.purge(accountId); // rows + media objects, no undo
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof AccountStateError) console.error("Disconnect it first");
}
```

## One agent for many tenants

A SaaS with 100 organizations doesn’t declare 100 agents. Register one definition and read the tenant from the conversation’s number: `instructions` and every tool receive `account`, and `account.tenantId` is the organization.

- `tenants?` is a list of tenant ids (`null` is the accounts without a tenant) or a predicate `(tenantId) => boolean`. Default: every tenant.
- It is combined with `accounts?`: an agent answers on a number only when **both** match.
- Only one agent may match a number. When both definitions use lists, Sendo checks `accounts` and `tenants` together at registration and throws `ConfigError` on an overlap; overlaps through predicates are logged when a message arrives and no agent answers on that number until you fix them.
- `mode?: "auto" | "draft"` is the definition’s default; a number’s own mode (`accounts.setAgentMode`) wins.
- No turn runs while the number (`accounts.pauseAgent`) or the conversation is paused. Inbound messages are still stored and still count as unread and open the 24-hour window. A turn already running when the pause is set finishes.

```ts title="ts"
// One definition for every organization: the tenant comes from the conversation's number.
const clinics = new Map([
  ["6f1c0c3e-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", { name: "Clínica Norte", hours: "8:00 a 17:00" }],
  ["6f1c0c3e-0000-4000-8000-000000000002", { name: "Clínica Sur", hours: "9:00 a 13:00" }],
]);

sendo.agent({
  name: "recepcion",
  tenants: (tenantId) => tenantId !== null && clinics.has(tenantId), // or a list of tenant ids
  model: "openai/gpt-5-mini",
  instructions: ({ account }) => {
    const clinic = clinics.get(account.tenantId ?? "");
    return `Eres la recepción de ${clinic?.name ?? "la clínica"}. Horario: ${clinic?.hours ?? "consultar"}.`;
  },
});

// A different agent for one organization: lists that overlap throw ConfigError at registration.
sendo.agent({
  name: "recepcion-pediatria",
  tenants: ["6f1c0c3e-0000-4000-8000-000000000003"],
  model: "openai/gpt-5-mini",
  instructions: "Eres la recepción de la clínica pediátrica.",
});
```

## Events

| Event                  | Payload                               | When                                                              |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `account.default`      | `{ tenantId, account, previous }`     | The tenant’s default changed; `account` is null when it has none. |
| `account.disconnected` | `{ account, by }`                     | A number was disconnected.                                        |
| `account.billing`      | `{ account, billingReady, previous }` | The payment-method state of a number changed.                     |

```ts title="ts"
sendo.on("account.default", ({ tenantId, account, previous }) => {
  console.log(
    "default of",
    tenantId,
    "is now",
    account?.displayPhone ?? "none",
    "was",
    previous?.id,
  );
});
sendo.on("account.disconnected", ({ account, by }) => {
  console.log(account.displayPhone, "disconnected by", by);
});
```

## Config

| Option        | Type                                          | Notes                                                                                                          |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `storeToken`  | `({ account, token }) => Promise<string>`     | Stores an Embedded Signup token, returns the `tokenRef`. Required for Embedded Signup.                         |
| `deleteToken` | `(tokenRef) => Promise<void>`                 | Called by `disconnect`.                                                                                        |
| `meta`        | `{ appId?, configId? }`                       | Your Meta app, for Embedded Signup.                                                                            |
| `coexistence` | `{ pauseAgentOnPhoneReply?, importHistory? }` | Numbers shared with the WhatsApp Business app ([Coexistence](https://sendo.dev/docs/coexistence)). Defaults `true` and `false`. |

## From the terminal

```shell
npx @quami/sendo accounts list [--tenant-id <uuid>] [--json]
npx @quami/sendo accounts set-default <id>
npx @quami/sendo accounts disconnect <id>
```

Details in [CLI](https://sendo.dev/docs/cli#accounts-manage).

Embedded Signup lets an admin connect a WhatsApp number from inside your app: Meta’s popup creates or picks the WhatsApp Business account and number, and Sendo finishes the setup server-side. Nobody copies ids or tokens by hand.

## What Meta requires first

> **Warning:**
>
> Embedded Signup runs on **your** Meta app, so your business becomes a Meta **Tech Provider**. Before the button works for customers, Meta requires:
>
> - **Business verification** of your Meta Business portfolio.
> - **App review** approving `whatsapp_business_management` and `whatsapp_business_messaging` (Advanced access).
> - Accepting the **Tech Provider** terms and creating an Embedded Signup configuration (the `configId`).
>
> This takes **days to weeks**, depending on Meta’s review queue. The manual path ([Meta setup](https://sendo.dev/docs/meta-setup) and `sendo accounts add`) needs none of it: a system user token on your own WABA works the same day.

Until the review passes, only people with a role on your Meta app can complete the popup; everyone else gets a permissions error (`META_PERMISSION_MISSING`, below).

## How it works

1. The admin taps **Conectar WhatsApp** on the numbers screen. The Facebook JS SDK opens Meta’s popup with `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENDO_META_APP_ID` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENDO_META_CONFIG_ID`.
2. Meta returns a single-use `code` and, from the session-info event, `waba_id` and `phone_number_id`. For a number that stays in the WhatsApp Business app, start the flow with `featureType: "whatsapp_business_app_onboarding"` ([Coexistence](https://sendo.dev/docs/coexistence)).
3. Your server action calls `sendo.accounts.completeEmbeddedSignup({ code, tenantId, wabaId, phoneNumberId, coexistence?, pin?, label? })`.

The `numbers` registry item ships the button, the action and a Supabase Vault `storeToken`. Requirements before the button: `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENDO_META_APP_ID`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENDO_META_CONFIG_ID`, `SENDO_META_APP_SECRET` and the `storeToken` / `deleteToken` hooks.

## The numbers screen

`npx shadcn add @sendo/numbers` installs **Números** at `/inbox/numbers`: connect (new number or the WhatsApp Business app flavour), label, default, agent pause and mode, **Ya lo agregué, verificar**, reconnect and disconnect (typing the last 4 digits). Only `admin` (`numbers.manage`) changes numbers; other roles get a read-only list, or nothing with `numbersVisibleToNonAdmins: false`.

- Implement `requireUser()` in `app/(protected)/inbox/numbers/numbers-config.ts` and `resolveRole` in `createSendo`.
- Apply `supabase/migrations/20261012000000_numbers_vault_tokens.sql` and uncomment `...createVaultTokenStore(supabaseAdmin)` and `meta` in `lib/sendo.ts`. Tokens are stored as `vault:<secret id>`; `resolveToken` reads those from Vault and every other `tokenRef` from `process.env`. Single-tenant apps can skip Vault and add their number with `sendo accounts add --token-env`.
- Add your HTTPS domain to the Meta app’s Facebook Login for Business settings (allowed JavaScript SDK domains).
- A `PIN_CONFLICT` spends the code, so the screen asks for the PIN and opens Meta’s popup again.
- **Token inválido · reconectar:** when the screen loads it re-checks every connected number with `refreshBilling`; Meta error 190 emits `account.error`, and those numbers are marked. Nothing extra is stored. Turn it off with `checkTokensOnLoad: false`.

## Configuration

`completeEmbeddedSignup` throws `ConfigError` without `storeToken`, `meta.appId` or the app secret. The business token goes to your secret store through `storeToken`; Sendo only saves the reference it returns as `tokenRef`, and your `resolveToken` reads it back.

```ts title="lib/sendo.ts"
// Keep the business token in your secret store; Sendo only saves the returned reference.
const storeToken: StoreToken = async ({ account, token }) => {
  const id = await saveSecret(
    `sendo/${account.tenantId ?? "default"}/${account.phoneNumberId}`,
    token,
  );
  return `vault:${id}`;
};
// Called on disconnect (and to undo a failed signup). Ignore refs you didn't create.
const deleteToken: DeleteToken = async (tokenRef) => {
  if (tokenRef.startsWith("vault:")) await deleteSecret(tokenRef.slice("vault:".length));
};

export const sendo = createSendo({
  adapter: memoryAdapter(), // your adapter
  appSecret: process.env.SENDO_META_APP_SECRET ?? "",
  verifyToken: process.env.SENDO_VERIFY_TOKEN ?? "",
  resolveToken: (account) =>
    account.tokenRef.startsWith("vault:")
      ? readSecret(account.tokenRef.slice("vault:".length))
      : process.env[account.tokenRef],
  storeToken,
  deleteToken,
  meta: {
    appId: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SENDO_META_APP_ID,
    configId: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SENDO_META_CONFIG_ID,
  },
});
```

## What completeEmbeddedSignup does

On Graph `GRAPH_API_VERSION`:

1. Exchanges the code: `GET /oauth/access_token?client_id&client_secret&code` → the business token.
2. Subscribes your app to the WABA’s webhooks: `POST /{waba_id}/subscribed_apps`.
3. Registers the number: `POST /{phone_number_id}/register` with the two-step PIN. Skipped for coexistence numbers. The PIN is the one the admin typed or a random 6 digits; it is never stored (two-step verification can be reset from WhatsApp Manager).
4. Reads `display_phone_number` and `verified_name`.
5. Calls `storeToken({ account, token })` → `tokenRef`.
6. Inserts the account with `onboarded_via = "embedded_signup"`, or reconnects the same row when the number was disconnected in the same tenant ([Numbers and tenants](https://sendo.dev/docs/numbers-and-tenants#disconnect)).
7. Makes it the tenant’s default if it is the tenant’s first number.
8. Checks billing (below).

A number already connected (in any tenant), or disconnected in another tenant, throws `DuplicateAccountError` before the code is used. If steps 1–5 fail, nothing is stored. If anything fails after `storeToken`, Sendo calls `deleteToken(tokenRef)`, unsubscribes the app from the WABA (best effort, skipped when another connected number uses the same WABA) and leaves no account row. The token never appears in logs, errors, rows or events.

## Errors

Branch on `code`; each one has a message your UI can show.

| `code`                    | Class                   | Meaning                                                                                                                             | Suggested message                                     |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| `SIGNUP_CODE_EXPIRED`     | `EmbeddedSignupError`   | The code expired or was already used.                                                                                               | “El enlace con Meta expiró, intenta de nuevo.”        |
| `META_PERMISSION_MISSING` | `EmbeddedSignupError`   | The app lacks a permission (app review not done) or access to the WABA/number.                                                      | Link to [the requirements](#requirements).            |
| `PIN_CONFLICT`            | `EmbeddedSignupError`   | The number already has a two-step PIN and it was missing or wrong (or too many attempts).                                           | Ask for the existing PIN and retry with `pin`.        |
| `SIGNUP_FAILED`           | `EmbeddedSignupError`   | Anything else; `step` says where (`exchange_code`, `subscribe_app`, `register_phone`, `read_phone`, `store_token`, `save_account`). | Generic retry.                                        |
| `ACCOUNT_EXISTS`          | `DuplicateAccountError` | The number is connected in another organization.                                                                                    | “Este número ya está conectado en otra organización.” |
| `CONFIG_ERROR`            | `ConfigError`           | Missing `storeToken`, `meta.appId` or app secret.                                                                                   | A setup problem, not a user one.                      |

`EmbeddedSignupError` also carries `step`, `metaCode`, `subcode`, `fbtraceId` and `details` from Meta. A popup the admin closed never reaches the server: show a neutral toast and create nothing.

```ts title="app/(protected)/numbers/actions.ts"
type ConnectResult = { ok: true; account: Account } | { ok: false; message: string };

/** Server action called by the "Conectar WhatsApp" button with what Meta returned. */
export async function connectWhatsApp(input: {
  tenantId: string;
  code: string;
  wabaId: string;
  phoneNumberId: string;
  coexistence?: boolean;
  pin?: string;
}): Promise<ConnectResult> {
  // Check your actor's `numbers.manage` capability first.
  try {
    const account = await sendo.accounts.completeEmbeddedSignup(input);
    return { ok: true, account };
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof DuplicateAccountError) {
      return { ok: false, message: "Este número ya está conectado en otra organización." };
    }
    if (error instanceof EmbeddedSignupError) {
      const messages: Record<EmbeddedSignupError["code"], string> = {
        SIGNUP_CODE_EXPIRED: "El enlace con Meta expiró, intenta de nuevo.",
        META_PERMISSION_MISSING:
          "Tu app de Meta aún no tiene los permisos necesarios. Revisa sendo.dev/docs/embedded-signup.",
        PIN_CONFLICT:
          "Este número tiene un PIN de verificación en dos pasos. Escríbelo para continuar.",
        SIGNUP_FAILED: "No pudimos conectar el número. Intenta de nuevo.",
      };
      return { ok: false, message: messages[error.code] };
    }
    throw error;
  }
}
```

## Billing state

As a Tech Provider you don’t pay for your customers’ messages: each customer adds a payment method to their own WABA in Meta. Until they do, sends fail. `account.billingReady` tracks it:

| Value   | Shown as                                 | Set by                                                                  |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `true`  | Conectado                                | Meta reports a payment method.                                          |
| `false` | Conectado · falta agregar método de pago | Meta reports none, or a send failed with error `131042`.                |
| `null`  | Conectado · estado de pago desconocido   | Not checked yet, or Meta didn’t say. The first failed send resolves it. |

- `sendo.accounts.refreshBilling(id)` reads the WABA’s funding field (`GET /{waba_id}?fields=primary_funding_id`): a funding id → `true`, none → `false`, a Meta error → the value is kept. It always sets `billingCheckedAt` and emits `account.billing` when the value changed. Wire it to a **Ya lo agregué, verificar** button.
- Sends never skip Meta because of `billingReady = false`: Meta is the source of truth. When Meta answers `131042`, Sendo sets `billingReady = false`, emits `account.billing` and rethrows the `MetaApiError` with `hint: "billing_not_ready"` (“Este número aún no tiene método de pago en Meta”).
- `sendo doctor` adds `account:<id>:billing`, a warning (not a failure) with a Meta Business Settings link when the value is `false` or `null`.

`MetaApiError.hint` is one of `billing_not_ready` (131042), `token_invalid` (190), `window_closed` (131047), `rate_limited` (4, 17, 32, 613, 80007, 130429, 131056), or `null`.

```ts title="ts"
export async function sendReminder(tenantId: string, phone: string): Promise<string | null> {
  try {
    await sendo.send.template(phone, "recordatorio_cita", { tenant: tenantId, language: "es" });
    return null;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof MetaApiError && error.hint === "billing_not_ready") {
      // Meta error 131042: the number is now billingReady = false and account.billing fired.
      return "Este número aún no tiene método de pago en Meta";
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

// "Ya lo agregué, verificar" on the numbers screen.
export async function verifyBilling(accountId: string) {
  const account = await sendo.accounts.refreshBilling(accountId);
  return account.billingReady; // true, false, or null (Meta didn't say)
}
```

Sendo measures the inbox from the rows it already stores; there is nothing to collect or schedule. Five SQL views (schema v3) define every number, `sendo.metrics.*` reads them with role checks, and the `metrics` registry item draws the screen supervisors and admins use.

## The API

Every method takes `{ actor, from, to, account?, tagIds? }`:

- `actor` comes from `sendo.actor(userId, tenantId)`. The tenant is always the actor's.
- `from` / `to` are a half-open range of instants. Build them from calendar days with `metricsDayRange({ from, to, timeZone })`: both days are included and the bounds are local midnights in that timezone.
- `account` narrows to one number (account id or Meta phone number id of the tenant; disconnected numbers keep their history). Another tenant's number is `ACCOUNT_NOT_FOUND`.
- `tagIds` is any-match on the conversation's current tags (the tag cut).

```ts title="ts"
// Supervisors and admins (`metrics.view`); agents get FORBIDDEN.
const actor: Actor = await sendo.actor(userId, tenantId);

// October, both days included, in the number's timezone (local midnights, DST-safe).
const range = metricsDayRange({
  from: "2026-10-01",
  to: "2026-10-31",
  timeZone: "America/Guatemala",
});

const daily = await sendo.metrics.daily({ actor, ...range }); // per day and number
const people = await sendo.metrics.byAssignee({ actor, ...range }); // names from resolveUsers
const firstResponse = await sendo.metrics.firstResponse({
  actor,
  ...range,
  account: phoneNumberId,
});
const resolved = await sendo.metrics.aiResolution({ actor, ...range, tagIds: [ventasTagId] });
const sources = await sendo.metrics.bySource({ actor, ...range });
// Admins only (`costs.view`): supervisors get FORBIDDEN here and `costCents: null` in `daily`.
const cost = await sendo.metrics.cost({ actor, ...range });
```

| Method          | Returns                                                                                                         | Needs          |
| --------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |
| `daily`         | Per day and number: `inbound`, `outbound`, `conversationsStarted`, `conversationsClosed`, `costCents`, tokens   | `metrics.view` |
| `byAssignee`    | Per person and day: `conversations`, `messagesSent`, `firstResponses`, `firstResponseSeconds[]`, median, `name` | `metrics.view` |
| `firstResponse` | Per conversation whose first inbound message is in range: who answered first and after how many `seconds`       | `metrics.view` |
| `aiResolution`  | Per conversation closed in range: `everHuman`, `humanMessages`, `agentMessages`, `aiResolved`                   | `metrics.view` |
| `bySource`      | New conversations per source and day (`source: null` = direct)                                                  | `metrics.view` |
| `cost`          | Model cost per AI agent and day: `costCents` (US cents), `tokensIn`, `tokensOut`, `turns`                       | `costs.view`   |

Supervisors and admins have `metrics.view`; agents get `FORBIDDEN`. Only admins have `costs.view`: for everyone else `cost` is `FORBIDDEN` and `daily` returns `costCents`, `tokensIn` and `tokensOut` as `null`. `byAssignee` adds `name` and `avatarUrl` from your `resolveUsers` (`null` when it isn't configured or doesn't know the id; a failing `resolveUsers` is logged and the names stay `null`). Phone replies from the WhatsApp Business app appear as `business_app`.

Medians are exact across days: merge the `firstResponseSeconds` arrays of the days you want and take the middle value (the mean of the two middle values for an even count), as the screen does.

## Definitions

- **Days** are calendar days in each number's timezone (`sendo_accounts.timezone`). A daily row counts when its local midnight is inside `[from, to)`. With numbers in several timezones, read one number at a time for exact days.
- **Messages** exclude internal notes, system lines, drafts and imported history. Inbound counts everything the contact sent; outbound counts what the agent, a person or the phone sent to the contact.
- **First response** is the first agent or human reply (not a note, not a draft) after the conversation's first inbound message.
- **Resolved by the AI** is a closed conversation that was never handed to a person (`ever_human = false`) and has no human message. Its close time is the last close system line, else the last activity.
- **Source** is `conversation.source` (`[#code]` from a link, `ad:<id>` from a Meta ad), counted when the conversation starts.
- **Cost** is the stored `cost_cents` of the agent's messages, drafts included (they cost the same to generate).

## The Métricas screen

```bash
npx shadcn add @sendo/metrics
```

The item adds `app/(protected)/inbox/metrics` with a date range (7, 30, 90 days or custom), number and tag filters, four KPI tiles (messages, new conversations, median first response, % resolved by the AI), a daily volume chart with a table view, tables per person, per source and per tag, and, for admins, cost per day and per agent. Implement `requireUser()` in `app/(protected)/inbox/metrics/metrics-config.ts` (it throws until you do) and configure `resolveRole`; the screen shows "No tienes acceso" to agents.

The chart is plain HTML and CSS (no chart library), with hover and keyboard tooltips and a table view; see `components/sendo/metrics/AGENTS.md` for the colours and how to change them.

## SQL for Drizzle and Prisma

Supabase apps get the five views from the `schema` migration v3 (`sendo_metrics_daily`, `sendo_metrics_by_assignee`, `sendo_metrics_first_response`, `sendo_metrics_ai_resolution`, `sendo_metrics_by_source`, all `security_invoker = true` so RLS applies). For any other Postgres client, `@quami/sendo` exports the same queries over the base tables as parameterized strings, so the numbers match the views exactly:

- `METRICS_SQL.daily`, `.byAssignee`, `.firstResponse`, `.aiResolution`, `.bySource`, `.cost`
- `metricsSqlParams({ tenantId, from, to, accountId?, tagIds? })` → `[$1 tenant, $2 from, $3 to, $4 account | null, $5 tag ids | null]`
- `METRICS_VIEWS`: the select body of each view, if you'd rather create the views yourself

```ts title="node-postgres or Drizzle"
// Any Postgres client that takes `$1…$5` parameters. Every query returns view rows (snake_case,
// one row per day × number × tag set): sum them per day yourself, or use `sendo.metrics.*`.
interface PgClient {
  query(text: string, params: unknown[]): Promise<{ rows: Record<string, unknown>[] }>;
}
declare const pool: PgClient; // node-postgres Pool, or Drizzle's `db.$client`

const params = metricsSqlParams({ tenantId, ...range, tagIds: [ventasTagId] });
const { rows: dailyRows } = await pool.query(METRICS_SQL.daily, params);
```

```ts title="Prisma"
interface PrismaLike {
  $queryRawUnsafe<T = unknown>(query: string, ...values: unknown[]): Promise<T>;
}
declare const prisma: PrismaLike;

const sourceRows = await prisma.$queryRawUnsafe<Record<string, unknown>[]>(
  METRICS_SQL.bySource,
  ...metricsSqlParams({ tenantId, ...range }),
);
```

```ts title="ts"
// The select bodies of the five `sendo_metrics_*` views (as the v3 migration creates them).
const ddl = Object.entries(METRICS_VIEWS)
  .map(
    ([name, body]) =>
      `create or replace view public.${name}\nwith (security_invoker = true) as\n${body};`,
  )
  .join("\n\n");
```

The queries return view rows in snake_case, one per day, number and tag set (so tag cuts stay exact). Sum them per day, or let the Drizzle and Prisma adapters do it: they run these queries behind `sendo.metrics.*`, so the API and the screen work the same on every adapter.

> **Note:**
>
> The SQL takes the tenant as a parameter and does no role check. Call it only after your own check, or use `sendo.metrics.*`, which checks `metrics.view` and `costs.view` for you.

A broadcast sends one APPROVED WhatsApp template to a list of contacts, a batch per minute, and keeps a result per recipient. Sendo decides who may receive it from the template's category and the contact's opt-in, and you can't override that: the preview tells you how many will get it and why the others are skipped, before anything is sent.

## Who receives a broadcast

| Template category               | Who receives it                                               | Skipped as  |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |
| `MARKETING`                     | Only contacts with `marketingOptIn = true` (an explicit yes). | `no_opt_in` |
| `UTILITY`, `AUTHENTICATION`     | Every contact who hasn't explicitly opted out.                | `opted_out` |
| Any category Sendo doesn't know | Treated as `MARKETING`.                                       | `no_opt_in` |

- **Opted out** means `marketingOptIn = false` **with** a decision date (`optInAt`): the contact said no, by keyword or through your app. A contact nobody asked yet has `optInAt = null`: they get utility messages ("tu cobro de noviembre fue emitido") but not promotions.
- Phones that don't parse are skipped as `invalid_phone` and never stored as contacts.
- Only APPROVED, active templates can be broadcast (`TEMPLATE_NOT_FOUND` otherwise). Sync them first with `sendo.templates.sync()`.
- Eligibility is checked again when each message is sent, so a contact who replies BAJA in the middle of a broadcast doesn't get it.

> **Note:**
>
> Pick the category honestly when you create the template in Meta. Meta re-categorizes templates whose content doesn't match, and marketing sent as utility can get the template paused.

## Opt-in and opt-out

Record a contact's explicit decision with `sendo.contacts.setOptIn(contactId, optIn, { source })`. It sets `marketingOptIn` and `optInAt`, and emits `contact.opt_in` (repeating the stored decision is a no-op).

```ts title="ts"
// The contact said yes in your signup form: MARKETING broadcasts may reach them now.
await sendo.contacts.setOptIn(patientContactId, true, { source: "form" });

sendo.on("contact.opt_in", ({ contact, optIn, source, keyword }) => {
  // source "keyword" + keyword "baja" when they replied BAJA; mirror it in your own records.
  console.log(contact.id, optIn, source, keyword);
});
```

**Opt-out keywords.** An inbound message that is exactly one of the keywords (default `BAJA`, `STOP`, `ALTO`, `CANCELAR`), after trimming and ignoring case and accents (`" Cancelár "` matches), opts the contact out: `marketingOptIn = false`, `contact.opt_in` with `source: "keyword"`, a confirmation message inside the 24-hour window (`optOut.confirmation`, default `BUILT_IN_MESSAGES[locale].optOutConfirmation`, so English unless `createSendo({ locale: "es" })`), and no agent turn for that message. "Quiero darme de baja" doesn't match; handle phrases in your agent or a rule if you want them.

```ts title="ts"
const sendo = createSendo({
  adapter: memoryAdapter(),
  appSecret: process.env.SENDO_META_APP_SECRET ?? "",
  verifyToken: process.env.SENDO_VERIFY_TOKEN ?? "",
  resolveToken: (account) => process.env[account.tokenRef],
  // Optional: these are the defaults. `keywords: []` turns keyword opt-out off.
  optOut: {
    keywords: ["BAJA", "STOP", "ALTO", "CANCELAR"],
    confirmation:
      "Listo, ya no te enviaremos mensajes promocionales. Si cambias de opinión, escríbenos.",
  },
});
```

Add the opt-out sentence to your marketing templates ("Responde BAJA si no quieres recibir promociones") so contacts know how.

## Preview, create and follow

Every `sendo.broadcasts` method takes an admin actor (`broadcasts.manage`). Recipients are contact ids, `{ phone }` (upserted as a contact on create) or either with its own `variables`, which win over the shared ones.

```ts title="ts"
const input = {
  actor: admin,
  template: "recordatorio_cita",
  language: "es",
  recipients: [
    { phone: "+502 5555 0101", variables: ["Ana", "lunes 12"] },
    { contactId: patientContactId, variables: ["Luis", "martes 13"] },
  ],
  ratePerMinute: 60,
};
const preview = await sendo.broadcasts.preview(input);
// { total: 2, eligible: 2, skipped: { no_opt_in: 0, opted_out: 0, invalid_phone: 0 },
//   category: "UTILITY", ratePerMinute: 60, estimatedMinutes: 1, accountId: "…" }
```

```ts title="ts"
const broadcast = await sendo.broadcasts.create(input); // status "queued"; the tick sends it
const progress = await sendo.broadcasts.get(broadcast.id, { actor: admin });
// progress.counts → { pending, sent, delivered, read, failed, skipped, cancelled }
const failed = await sendo.broadcasts.recipients(broadcast.id, { actor: admin, status: "failed" });
// failed.items[0].error → { code: 131026, title: "Message undeliverable", message: "…" }
await sendo.broadcasts.cancel(broadcast.id, { actor: admin }); // pending → cancelled
```

- `create` stores the broadcast as `queued` and each recipient as `pending` or `skipped` with the reason. Nothing is sent yet.
- **The tick sends it.** `sendo.maintenance.tick()` (every minute, see [the tick](https://sendo.dev/docs/workflows#tick)) sends up to `ratePerMinute` pending recipients per broadcast (default 60), using the same conversation as any other message. A broadcast never moves a conversation to `human` and never runs the agent for the template; replies land in the normal conversation and the inbox.
- Status webhooks move each recipient to `sent`, `delivered`, `read` or `failed` (with Meta's error). The broadcast ends `done` when nothing is pending.
- `cancel(id)` turns the pending recipients into `cancelled`. Messages already sent stay sent.

## Billing pauses and limits

Meta bills every delivered message directly to the WhatsApp Business account that owns the number, by category and the recipient's country. Sendo moves no money and never copies Meta's rates: see [Meta's official pricing](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/whatsapp/pricing).

- **No payment method (Meta 131042):** the broadcast pauses with `pauseReason: "billing"` and the number's `billingReady` becomes `false`. Pending recipients stay pending. Once the business adds a payment method and Meta confirms it (the tick re-checks billing hourly, or call `sendo.accounts.refreshBilling(id)`), the next tick resumes the broadcast by itself.
- **Rate or tier limits (130429 and the other rate-limit codes):** that minute's pass stops and the recipient stays pending; the next tick continues. Meta also caps how many unique contacts a number can reach per day by its messaging tier.
- **Template no longer approved:** the broadcast stops as `failed` and its pending recipients are cancelled.

## The Envíos screen (`broadcasts`)

```shell
npx shadcn add @sendo/broadcasts
```

The "Envíos" screen at `/inbox/broadcasts` (admins only):

1. **Nuevo envío:** pick a number, an approved template (with its preview and the category rule), its variables, one of your app's contact lists or filters, and the rate.
2. **Revisar envío:** total, eligible and skipped counts by reason, the estimated time, and the cost note linking to Meta's pricing. "Enviar a N contactos" is disabled when nobody is eligible.
3. **Progress:** counts per state (refreshed every 5 seconds while sending), per-recipient results with Meta's error on failed rows, "Cancelar envío", and a notice with a link to add a payment method when the broadcast is paused for billing.

Before using it, edit `app/(protected)/inbox/broadcasts/broadcasts-config.ts`: implement `requireUser()` (it throws until you do) and return your audiences from `broadcastAudiences`. Recipients are always resolved on the server from the audience id; the browser never sends a phone list. The screen also needs the tick route from `workflows`. Details in the item's `AGENTS.md`.
